-- title:
--   Node stalks of a Cartier quadruple: lattices and kernel lines
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime, $k$ an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$, and $\bar k = \mathrm{WittVector}\ p\ k / p$ denotes the quotient of the Witt vectors of $k$ by the ideal `pIdeal`, generated by $p$. Here `Zp2 p` is the Witt ring $W(\mathbb F_{p^2})$, and a `FormalODModule p B` consists of a two-dimensional commutative formal group law $F$ over $B$ together with an action of `Zp2 p` by endomorphism series and a series $\varpi$ satisfying $\varpi\circ\varpi = [p]$ and $\varpi\circ[a] = [\sigma a]\circ\varpi$.
--
--   **The special formal module $\Phi$ over $\bar k$.** Given a ring homomorphism $\iota :$ `Zp2 p` $\to W(k)$, write $\bar\jmath$ for its reduction, the composite of $\iota$ with the quotient map $W(k) \to \bar k$. Let $\Phi$ be a `FormalODModule p` over $\bar k$ subject to: `hΦ`, that $\Phi$ is special for $\bar\jmath$, i.e. the submodules `lieZero` and `lieOne` of the Lie module are complementary and each invertible over $\bar k$; `hΦ4`, that $\Phi$ has height $4$, i.e. the action of $p$ has kernel of degree $p^4$; `h0Φ`, that `lieZero` for $\bar\jmath$ is contained in the kernel of `lieVarpi`, the linear map given by the linear part of $\varpi$ on the Lie module; and `hcΦ`, that the graded pieces of index $0$ and $1$ of the Cartier module of $\Phi$ for $\bar\jmath$ are complementary, where the graded piece of index $n$ consists of those Cartier module elements $f$ with $[\tau(c)]f = \bar\jmath(\tau(c))^{p^n}\cdot f$ for all $c \in \mathbb F_{p^2}$, $\tau$ the Teichmüller lift.
--
--   **The rigidification $r_\Phi$.** By `hcΦ` the Cartier module of $\Phi$ becomes a `GradedCartierModuleData`; $r_\Phi$ is an additive map from $\mathbb Z_p^2 = (\mathrm{Fin}\ 2 \to \mathbb Z_p)$ to its associated module `NMod`. The hypothesis `hrΦ` requires that for every additive map $L$ from the Cartier module to `NMod` which is a canonical $L$-map (the predicate `IsCanonicalLMap`: $L$ is a Cartier $L$-map and lifts, along a surjection from a special graded Cartier module datum over a $p$-torsion-free ring, to a Cartier $L$-map there), $r_\Phi$ maps all of $\mathbb Z_p^2$ bijectively onto the degree-$0$ piece `etaPiece` $L$ $0$.
--
--   **The matrix $g$.** $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb Q_p)$ is assumed, by `hg`, to be the diagonal matrix $\mathrm{diag}(p,1)$.
--
--   **The edge family $X$.** `EdgeFamily.edgeRingCharP p` $\bar k$ is the chart ring `FormalOmega.chartERing` $\bar k$ at $0$; $X$ is a `FormalODModule p` over it, and $\gamma : \mathrm{Fin}\ 2 \to$ `CartierModule p X.F`. With respect to the structure map `structureMap` $\iota$ obtained from the composite of the reduction $W(k) \to \bar k$ with the structural algebra map $\bar k \to$ `edgeRingCharP p` $\bar k$, the hypotheses are: `hγ`, that $\gamma$ is a homogeneous $V$-basis, i.e. $\gamma i$ lies in the graded piece of index $i$ and the determinant of the matrix of tangents of the $\gamma i$ is a unit; `hγa`, that $X$ has structure constants `EdgeFamily.edgeRingConstants p` $\bar k$, i.e. for each $i$ and each $N$ the element $\varpi\cdot\gamma i$ agrees, modulo $V^N$, with the prescribed $V$-expansion $\sum_{m<N} V^m([a_{m,i}]\gamma_{\pi(m,i)})$; `hXs`, that $X$ is special for the same structure map; and `hX4`, that $X$ has height $4$.
--
--   **The node point $f_0$.** $f_0$ is a ring homomorphism from `edgeRingCharP p` $\bar k$ to $\bar k$ which splits the structural algebra map (`hf₀`) and annihilates both chart coordinates $\xi$ and $\eta$ (`hf₀ξ`, `hf₀η`).
--
--   **The normalised node isogeny.** For $m \in \mathbb N$ and $\rho_0$ a $2$-tuple of formal power series over $\bar k$, `hρ₀` asserts that $\rho_0$ is an isogeny $\Phi \to X\otimes_{f_0}\bar k$ of height $4m$, i.e. an $O_D$-homomorphism whose kernel has degree $p^{4m}$. Let $t^0 :=$ `Rigidified.mk` $(X\otimes_{f_0}\bar k)\ m\ \bar\rho_0$ be the resulting rigidified triple over $\bar k$, $\bar\rho_0$ being the reduction of $\rho_0$ modulo $p$. Then `hOD₀` asserts that `Rigidified.ρ` of $t^0$ is an $O_D$-homomorphism from `Rigidified.Φbar` of $t^0$ to `Rigidified.Xbar` of $t^0$; `hcb` (`Rigidified.IsGradedSbar` along the identity of $\bar k$) asserts complementarity of the graded pieces of index $0$ and $1$ of `Rigidified.XbarS` of $t^0$ for `Rigidified.jSbar`; and `hcΦg` (`Rigidified.IsGradedPhiS`) the corresponding complementarity for the base change of $\Phi$.
--
--   **Normalisation of the rigidification on the $\eta$-basis.** For $a \in \mathbb N$, the hypothesis `hN` states that for each $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\ 2$,
--   $$p^a\cdot \mathrm{rigidNum}(\mathrm{Pi.single}\ i\ 1) = p^{a+m}\cdot v_i,$$
--   where `Rigidified.rigidNum` $\iota$ `hcΦ` $r_\Phi$ is the transport of $r_\Phi$ into the `NMod` of the graded Cartier module datum of `Rigidified.XbarS` of $t^0$, obtained by composing $r_\Phi$ with the `nMap` induced by the base change of $\Phi$ and then with the `nMap` induced by `Rigidified.ρ`, and where $v_0 =$ `nMk` of the pair $(\gamma_0', 0)$ and $v_1 =$ `nVarpi` applied to `nMk` of the pair $(\gamma_1', 0)$, with $\gamma_i'$ the image of $\gamma i$ under the successive Cartier module base changes along $f_0$ and the reductions modulo $p$.
--
--   **The geometric point and the quadruple.** Let $\Omega$ be an algebraically closed field which is a $\mathbb Z_p$-algebra, $y_0 : \bar k \to \Omega$ a ring homomorphism, and $Q$ a `DrinfeldDatum` over $\Omega$ for the field $\mathbb Q_p$, the ring $\mathbb Z_p$ and the uniformiser $p$ (so $Q$ consists of two families of full $\mathbb Z_p$-lattices $N_0(x) \le N_1(x)$ in $\mathbb Q_p^2$ indexed by the primes of $\Omega$, invertible $\Omega$-modules $T_0, T_1$ with maps $\Pi_0, \Pi_1$ composing to multiplication by $p$, and surjections $u_0(x)$, $u_1(x)$ from the base changes of the lattices to the stalks, subject to the compatibilities of the structure). The hypothesis `hQ` asserts `IsCartierQuadruple` $\iota$ `hcΦ` $r_\Phi$ for the base change of $t^0$ along $y_0$ and for $Q$: that `Rigidified.ρ` is an $O_D$-homomorphism, and that there are identifications of $T_0$, $T_1$ with the Lie pieces of the rigidified module compatible with $\Pi_0$, $\Pi_1$ and `lieVarpi`, together with clauses (summarised here) describing, at each prime, membership in $N_0(x)$ and $N_1(x)$ and the maps $u_0(x)$, $u_1(x)$ in terms of $\eta$-sections of the associated graded Cartier module data over localisations. Finally $x$ is a prime of $\Omega$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** There exist an identity $h_0$ of submodules $N_0(x) =$ `stdLattice` $\mathbb Z_p\ \mathbb Q_p$, the underlying lattice of `stdFullLattice`, and an identity $h_1$ of submodules $N_1(x) =$ the underlying lattice of `FullLattice.act` of the scalar matrix $\mathrm{scalarGL}$ attached to the inverse of the unit $p$ of $\mathbb Q_p$ applied to `FullLattice.act` $g$ `stdFullLattice` — that is, $N_1(x) = p^{-1}g\,\mathbb Z_p^2 = \mathbb Z_p e_0 \oplus p^{-1}\mathbb Z_p e_1$ — such that both of the following hold, over the local ring `locRing` $\Omega\ x$ at $x$:
--
--   1.
--
--   The kernel of $u_0(x)$ is the free rank-one submodule spanned by the image, under `transportEquiv` along $h_0^{-1}$, of $0 \otimes e_0 + 1 \otimes e_1$, where $e_0, e_1$ are the standard basis vectors `stdBasisVec` of the standard full lattice.
--
--   2.
--
--   The kernel of $u_1(x)$ is the free rank-one submodule spanned by the image, under `transportEquiv` along $h_1^{-1}$, of the element obtained from $1 \otimes e_0 + 0 \otimes e_1$ by applying successively the base-changed lattice isomorphisms `actBaseChange` for $g$ and then for the scalar matrix attached to the inverse of the unit $p$.
--
--   This is the local computation, at the crossing point of the edge family, identifying the stalks of an arbitrary Cartier quadruple of the normalised node triple with those of the node of the standard edge of the Bruhat–Tits tree of $\mathrm{PGL}_2(\mathbb Q_p)$: the lattice pair $\mathbb Z_p^2 \subset p^{-1}\mathrm{diag}(p,1)\mathbb Z_p^2$ together with the two coordinate kernel lines. It is stated in exactly the shape required by the node recognition criterion for Drinfeld data, and is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.forall_isCartierQuadruple_map_node_line_eq_of_rigidNum_single_eq`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.forall_isCartierQuadruple_map_node_line_eq_of_rigidNum_single_eq) in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation of the relevant Shimura curves; it is obtained by combining the lattice identification `lattice_eq_of_isCartierQuadruple_map_node_of_rigidNum_single_eq` with the kernel computation `ker_eq_span_of_lattice_eq_of_isCartierQuadruple_map_node_of_rigidNum_single_eq`.
